dispatchers each route stop’s status and last-updated time.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 6,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Show each route box’s latest available temperature, status, and gateway alerts, and mark cached temperature data as stale while disconnected.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 7,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"For scanned boxes on hold, display “Do not deliver” and the returned hold reason, and prevent handover of those boxes.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 8,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"When a scanned box is recalled according to the service or offline cache, display “Do not deliver” and prevent its handover.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 9,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Let drivers record eligible handovers with receiver name, signature, delivery photo, actual handover time, and available browser location.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 10,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Allow otherwise eligible handovers when browser location is denied or unavailable, and record location as unavailable.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 11,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"While offline, retain loading, handover, and refusal work locally across session expiry or sign-out; isolate queued work to its originating account; require that user to re-authenticate before syncing; automatically send queued work when connectivity returns; preserve handover occurrence time and identifier on retries; clear payloads only after successful acknowledgement; and retain permanently rejected payloads for user review before retry. If local storage cannot retain a complete payload, show an error and do not report the work as queued.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 12,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"When an assigned route changes while a driver is offline, show route differences, require review and resolution of affected unsent work before continuing, and preserve completed offline handovers.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 13,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"When an offline handover conflicts with a hold or recall during synchronization, retain and flag the handover and display the returned quality-case result.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 14,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Present a single-person quality-decision flow only when an excursion is within the first tier above or below the permitted band, allowance remains, and the proposed decision matches the recommendation. Otherwise require a Quality Officer proposal and separate approval by a different Quality Manager. Require written justification when a decision opposes the recommendation.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 15,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Require identity confirmation using the account’s sign-in method and a reason when a user signs a quality decision; present signed decisions as read-only with a separate superseding-decision action.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 16,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"When the service returns a quality disposition, show resulting stock, order, and route status and, when superseded, the earlier and current disposition.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 17,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Collect destruction date, quantity, and witness for rejected stock, and prevent entry of a quantity greater than the displayed rejected quantity.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 18,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Identify decisions waiting more than four hours as escalated in the Quality Manager’s waiting-decisions view.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 19,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Provide a Quality Manager flow to start and close recalls with signed actions; recall start collects product, one or more lots, reason, and severity, and the app displays each returned action result.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 20,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Display the returned recall trace, including warehouse locations, boxes, routes, customer deliveries, quantities, delivery dates, receivers, and the trace total alongside received quantity when supplied.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 21,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Show customer recall notices in the portal, link notices to affected deliveries, and provide a response entry point.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 22,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Show customers only their organisation’s current and past orders, allocated lots and expiry dates, order status, and in-transit delivery arrival and temperature status, without exposing other route customers.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 23,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Provide a PDF certificate for each completed delivery in the signed-in customer’s organisation, containing products, lots, expiry dates, quantities, receiver, handover time, each box’s temperature chart from sealing to handover, excursions, and signed excursion decisions.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 24,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"When late readings produce a new certificate version, list the new and earlier versions for the signed-in customer’s completed delivery and keep each available for download.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 25,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Present the customer portal and certificates in the user’s selected English or Arabic, render Arabic right to left including tables and charts, and show dates and times in the account’s configured time zone.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 26,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Provide an audit-trail screen showing returned entries with actor, time, changed fields, old and new values, and supplied reason; support filters by record, user, date range, and action type, and export the filtered view.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 27,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Show the returned history of a lot from receipt through delivery or destruction in time order.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 28,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Provide on-screen reports for selected date ranges and warehouses, with CSV export of displayed figures, covering excursion counts and rates by route, driver, vehicle, and shipper box type; packs rejected and destroyed by product and cause; on-time deliveries within 60 minutes of planned arrival and in-full deliveries when every ordered pack is handed over, each as a percentage; average time from excursion to decision; stock within 60 days of expiry by product and warehouse; and open recalls and their progress.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 29,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Make the web app responsive on phone, tablet, and desktop browsers, with touch-sized controls on narrow screens.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 30,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Support the latest two browser versions of Chrome, Edge, Safari, and Firefox.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 31,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Conform the main web-app flows to WCAG 2.1 AA.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 32,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Render the full recall trace returned by the service for the assumed launch scale.\"\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 33,\n  \"kind\": \"not asked for\",\n  \"problem\": \"Support the assumed first-year product scale and make a requested lot trace available through its screen within the requested time limit.\"\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\n<artifact id=\"spec\" kind=\"spec\" sha=\"\">\n{\n \"requirements\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-1\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian responsive web app shall provide individual email-and-password sign-in and self-service password reset for driver, staff, and customer areas.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-2\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide System Administrator screens to create and deactivate accounts and assign roles, staff warehouse access, and customer-organisation access.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-3\",\n   \"ears\": \"When a driver opens today's routes, the Meridian web app shall show only that driver's assigned routes and each route's stops in route order.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-4\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall compare scanned shipper boxes with expected boxes before route departure, report missing or wrong-route boxes, and keep departure blocked until all expected boxes are scanned and wrong boxes are removed or corrected.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-5\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show dispatchers each route stop's status and last-updated time.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-6\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show each route box's latest available temperature, status, and alerts from the supplied gateway data and identify cached temperature data as stale while disconnected.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-7\",\n   \"ears\": \"When scanned stock is On Hold, the Meridian web app shall display “Do not deliver” and the returned hold reason and prevent handover of that box.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-8\",\n   \"ears\": \"When the service or an offline cache reports a scanned box as Recalled, the Meridian web app shall display “Do not deliver” and prevent handover of that box.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-9\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall let drivers record an eligible handover with receiver name, signature, delivery photo, actual handover time, and available browser location.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-10\",\n   \"ears\": \"If browser location is denied or unavailable, then the Meridian web app shall allow an otherwise eligible handover and record location as unavailable.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-11\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall let drivers record full or partial refusal only by whole box and require a reason for each refused box.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-12\",\n   \"ears\": \"While the browser is offline, the Meridian web app shall retain loading, handover, and refusal work locally across session expiry or sign-out, isolate queued work to the originating account so another signed-in user cannot view or send it, require the originating user to re-authenticate before syncing, automatically send queued work when connectivity returns, preserve handover occurrence time and identifier on retries, clear queued payload only after successful service acknowledgement, show an error without reporting work as queued when local storage cannot retain the complete payload, retain permanently rejected payloads and show the returned error while requiring user review before retry, and keep another account's queued payload inaccessible and unsent until the originating account re-authenticates.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-13\",\n   \"ears\": \"When an assigned route changes while a driver is offline, the Meridian web app shall show route differences, require review and resolution of affected unsent work before continuing, and preserve completed offline handovers.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-14\",\n   \"ears\": \"When an offline handover conflicts with a hold or recall upon synchronization, the Meridian web app shall retain and flag the handover and display the returned quality-case result.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-15\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show Quality reviewers the full excursion temperature history, allowance used and remaining for each tier, and the returned recommendation, using the configured tier rules.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-16\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall present a single-person quality-decision flow only when an excursion is within the first tier above or below the permitted band, allowance remains, and the proposed decision matches the recommendation, and otherwise present a Quality Officer proposal and separate approval by a different Quality Manager; a written justification is required when a decision opposes the recommendation.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-17\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall require identity confirmation using the account's sign-in method and a reason when a user signs a quality decision, and present signed decisions as read-only with a separate superseding-decision action.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-18\",\n   \"ears\": \"When the service returns a quality disposition, the Meridian web app shall show resulting stock, order, and route status and the earlier and current disposition when superseded.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-19\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall collect destruction date, quantity, and witness for rejected stock and prevent entry of quantity greater than the displayed rejected quantity.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-20\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall identify quality decisions waiting more than four hours as escalated in the Quality Manager's waiting-decisions view.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-21\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide a Quality Manager flow to start and close recalls with signed actions, collecting product, one or more lots, reason, and severity for recall start, and display each returned action result.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-22\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall display the returned recall trace, including warehouse locations, boxes, routes, customer deliveries, quantities, delivery dates, receivers, and the trace total alongside received quantity when supplied.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-23\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show customer recall notices in the portal, link notices to affected deliveries, and provide a response entry point.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-24\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall let customers submit or update per-delivery on-hand, used or dispensed, and destroyed quantities, allow a total below the delivered amount as unaccounted, and reject a total above it.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-25\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show recall progress as the returned percentage accounted for, identify outstanding customers and reminder status when supplied, and let a Quality Manager submit a signed write-off justification for unaccounted quantity.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-26\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show customers only their organisation's current and past orders, allocated lots and expiry dates, order status, and in-transit delivery arrival and temperature status, without exposing other route customers.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-27\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide a PDF certificate for each completed delivery in the signed-in customer's organisation with products, lots, expiry dates, quantities, receiver, handover time, each box's temperature chart from sealing to handover, excursions, and signed excursion decisions.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-28\",\n   \"ears\": \"When late readings produce a new certificate version, the Meridian web app shall list the new and earlier versions for the signed-in customer's completed delivery and keep each available for download.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-29\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall present the customer portal and certificates in the user's selected English or Arabic, render Arabic right to left including tables and charts, and show dates and times in the account's configured time zone.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-30\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide an audit-trail screen showing returned entries with actor, time, changed fields, old and new values, and supplied reason, with filters by record, user, date range, and action type and export of the filtered view.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-31\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall show the returned history of a lot from receipt through delivery or destruction in time order.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-32\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide on-screen reports for selected date ranges and warehouses, with CSV export of displayed figures, including excursion counts and rates by route, driver, vehicle, and shipper box type; packs rejected and destroyed by product and cause; deliveries on time within 60 minutes of planned arrival and in full when every ordered pack is handed over, each as a percentage; average time from excursion to decision; stock within 60 days of expiry by product and warehouse; and open recalls and their progress.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-33\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall provide forms to enter opening lots and stock records and submit them to the service.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"REQ-34\",\n   \"ears\": \"The Meridian web app shall make recorded handover signatures and delivery photos available from a delivery record within its seven-year evidence-retention period.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"nfrs\": [\n  \"The Meridian web app shall be responsive on phone, tablet, and desktop browsers, with touch-sized controls on narrow screens.\",\n  \"The Meridian web app shall support the latest two browser versions of Chrome, Edge, Safari, and Firefox.\",\n  \"The main web-app flows shall conform to WCAG 2.1 AA.\",\n  \"The web app shall render the full recall trace returned by the service for the assumed launch scale.\",\n  \"The web client shall support the assumed first-year product scale and make a requested lot trace available through its screen within the requested time limit.\"\n ],\n \"outOfScope\": [\n  \"API-side enforcement of credentials, password-reset token validity/delivery, role and access-scope authorization, and account state; web requirements cover forms, requests, screens, and API response handling.\",\n  \"API-side persistence and enforcement of stock/order/route transitions, hold and recall blocking across warehouses, quarantine, quantity validation/conservation, recall trace completeness and arithmetic, recall closure eligibility, write-off authorization, and duplicate-handover idempotency.\",\n  \"API-side audit creation, tamper detection, immutability, seven-year retention of audit records and handover evidence, and certificate-version persistence.\",\n  \"API-side scheduled and outbound emails, including recall notices and 24-hour reminders, four-hour decision escalation, daily summaries, monthly reports, and monthly report generation; web screens display supplied results/status where specified.\",\n  \"API-side production of recall traces within a time limit; the client timing requirements measure rendering after the response or from the user's request, as specified in separate requirements.\",\n  \"Supplying live temperature readings, alerts, box associations, and history through the existing logger or supplier gateway feed.\",\n  \"Importing or guaranteeing initial database contents; the web app provides opening-lot and stock entry.\",\n  \"Pricing, invoicing and payments; route stop-order optimization; manufacturer purchasing and demand forecasting; automatic driver or customer penalties; stock transfers between warehouses; logger hardware and supplier gateway implementation.\",\n  \"Native iOS or Android applications, app-store distribution, push notifications, and background location. The product is a responsive web app; applicable notices use in-app notices or email, location uses the browser prompt, and the web app may be installable.\"\n ]\n}\n</artifact>\n\n<untrusted_document id=\"request\" source=\"cli\">\nThe driver sees today's route with its stops in order. Before leaving, the driver scans each shipper box\nonto the vehicle. The application warns about a box that belongs to another route or a box that is\nmissing.\n\nDuring the route the application shows the current temperature and status of each box and any alerts.\n\nAt a stop the driver scans the boxes for that stop. If a box holds stock that is On Hold, the application\nshows \"Do not deliver\" for that box and does not allow it to be handed over. For the remaining boxes,\nthe driver records the receiver's name, captures their signature and a photo of the delivered boxes,\nand completes the handover. The time and location are recorded automatically.\n\nA receiver may refuse all or part of a delivery. The driver records what was refused and a reason, and\nrefused boxes return to the warehouse, where they are Quarantined.\n\nEverything above shall work without a connection. Work done offline is sent when the connection\nreturns. If the route was changed while the driver was offline, the driver is shown the difference before\ncontinuing, and a handover already completed offline is never lost or undone.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● The driver sees only their own routes.\n● Loading requires every box on the route to be scanned, and a wrong or missing box is reported.\n● A box with stock On Hold cannot be handed over, and the driver sees the reason.\n● A handover records receiver name, signature, photo, time and location.\n● A partial or full refusal can be recorded with a reason per box.\n● Loading, handover and refusal work without a connection and are sent automatically later.\n● A handover recorded offline keeps the time it actually happened, not the time it was sent.\n● If a hold was placed on a box while the driver was offline and the box was handed over, the\nhandover is kept, the conflict is flagged and a quality case is opened.\n● The same handover sent twice is recorded once.\n● The dispatcher sees each stop's status and the time it was last updated.\n\nUS-010: Decide What Happens to Affected Stock\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Quality Manager, I want every excursion to end in a recorded, signed decision so that no\naffected product reaches a patient without a responsible person approving it.\n\nDescription\n\nEvery excursion requires a decision for each affected handling unit: Release or Reject.\n\nThe system shows the reviewer the full temperature history, the budget used and remaining per tier,\nand a recommendation: Release when every tier still has allowance left, Reject when any tier is used\nup or a not-permitted range was reached.\n\n● If the excursion stayed within the first tier above or below the band and allowance remains, a\nQuality Officer may decide alone.\n● In every other case, a Quality Officer proposes and a Quality Manager approves. The two shall be\ndifferent people.\n● A decision that goes against the system's recommendation always needs both, and a written\njustification.\n\nEach decision is signed electronically: the person confirms their identity again at the moment of\nsigning and gives a reason. A signed decision cannot be edited. It can only be superseded by a new\nsigned decision, and both remain on record.\n\nRejected stock is removed from available stock and listed for destruction. Destruction is recorded\nwith a date, a quantity and a witness.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● Stock that is On Hold cannot be allocated, packed, loaded or handed over.\n● The reviewer sees the history, the budget per tier and the system's recommendation.\n● A single-person decision is allowed only in the case described above.\n● The person who proposes a decision cannot approve it, even if they hold both roles.\n● Signing requires the person to confirm their identity again and enter a reason.\n● A signed decision cannot be changed or deleted by anyone, including a System Administrator.\n● Releasing stock returns it to where it was: available, or back on its order and route.\n● Rejecting stock removes it from its order, and the order is reallocated under US-005.\n● Decisions waiting longer than 4 hours are escalated to the Quality Manager.\n● Destroyed quantities can never exceed rejected quantities.\n\nUS-011: Recall a Lot\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Quality Manager, I want to recall a lot and see every unit accounted for so that no recalled\nproduct stays in use.\n\nDescription\n\nA Quality Manager starts a recall for a product and one or more lot numbers, with a reason and a\nseverity.\n\nStarting a recall immediately:\n\n● Sets all stock of those lots that Meridian holds, in any warehouse, to Recalled.\n● Removes it from every order and stops it being handed over, including boxes already on a van.\n● Lists every customer who received any of those lots, with quantities, delivery dates and receivers.\n● Notifies those customers by email and in the customer portal.\n\nThe trace from lot to every location and customer shall be available within one minute of the request.\n\nEach affected customer responds in the portal with, for each delivery, how many packs are still on\nhand, how many were used or dispensed, and how many were destroyed on site. Packs on hand are\ncollected on a return route and Quarantined on arrival.\n\nA recall can be closed only when every pack of the lot is accounted for: held by Meridian, returned,\nreported used, reported destroyed, or written off as unaccounted with a signed justification.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● Only a Quality Manager can start or close a recall, and both actions are signed.\n● Recalled stock is blocked across all warehouses at once, including stock that is packed or on a\nroute.\n● A driver on a route sees \"Do not deliver\" for a recalled box as soon as the device is online.\n● The trace lists every warehouse location, box, route and customer delivery for the lot, with\nquantities.\n● The quantities in the trace add up exactly to the total quantity received for the lot.\n● Customers are notified and can respond per delivery.\n● A customer's reported quantities cannot exceed what was delivered to them.\n● Customers who have not responded within 24 hours are reminded, and the Quality Manager sees\nwho is outstanding.\n● Recall progress shows the percentage of packs accounted for.\n● A recall cannot be closed while any pack is unaccounted for without a signed write-off.\n\nUS-012: Follow Orders in the Customer Portal\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Customer User, I want to follow my deliveries and download proof of their condition so that\nI can accept stock with confidence.\n\nDescription\n\nThe customer portal shows the organisation's orders, their status, the lots and expiry dates allocated,\nand for deliveries on the road the planned arrival time and whether the temperature is in range.\n\nAfter delivery, the customer can download a delivery certificate as a PDF for each delivery. The\ncertificate shows the products, lots, expiry dates and quantities, the receiver and time of handover,\nthe temperature chart for each box from sealing to handover, any excursions and the signed decisions\non them.\n\nThe portal and the certificate are available in English and Arabic according to the user's preference.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● A customer sees current and past orders of their own organisation only.\n● A delivery on the road shows its planned arrival time and an in-range or out-of-range status,\nwithout revealing other customers on the route.\n● A delivery certificate can be downloaded for every completed delivery.\n● The certificate contains the contents by lot, the handover details, the temperature chart, and any\nexcursions with their decisions.\n● A certificate issued once does not change. If late readings alter the history, a new version is\nissued and both remain available.\n● All dates and times are shown in the customer's own time zone.\n● Switching to Arabic turns the whole portal right to left, including tables, charts and the certificate.\n\nUS-013: Audit Trail\n\nUser Story\n\nAs an Auditor, I want a complete, unalterable record of who did what and when so that Meridian\ncan demonstrate compliance during an inspection.\n\nDescription\n\nThe system shall record every creation, change and deletion of regulated data: products and profiles,\nstock, orders and allocations, boxes and routes, handovers, excursions, decisions, recalls, users and\nroles, and configuration.\n\nEach entry records who, when, what was changed, the old and new values, and the reason where one\nwas given. Actions taken by the system itself, such as automatic holds and allocations, are recorded\nas such.\n\nAudit entries cannot be edited or deleted by anyone. It shall be possible to demonstrate that the trail\nhas not been altered.\n\nAll times are stored in a single reference time and shown in the viewer's time zone. Karachi and\nLahore share one time zone and Dubai is in another.\n\nRecords shall be kept for 7 years.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● Every change to regulated data produces an audit entry with who, when, old value, new value and\nreason.\n● Automatic actions are recorded and attributed to the system.\n● No user, including a System Administrator, can edit or delete an audit entry.\n● An alteration made to the audit trail outside the application can be detected.\n● The audit trail can be filtered by record, user, date range and type of action.\n● An Auditor can export any filtered view.\n● The full history of one lot, from receipt to delivery or destruction, can be viewed in one place in\ntime order.\n\nUS-014: Reports and Scheduled Summaries\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Quality Manager, I want regular reports on excursions, wastage and delivery performance so\nthat I can find and fix weak points in the cold chain.\n\nDescription\n\nA daily summary shall be emailed at 07:00 warehouse time to the Quality Officers of each warehouse,\nlisting excursions in the last 24 hours, decisions still waiting, silent devices and stock On Hold.\n\nA monthly report shall be generated on the first day of each month and emailed to configured\nstakeholders. It should include, at a minimum:\n\n● Number and rate of excursions by route, driver, vehicle and shipper box type.\n● Packs rejected and destroyed, by product and by cause.\n● Deliveries on time and in full, as a percentage.\n● Average time from excursion to decision.\n● Stock within 60 days of expiry, by product and warehouse.\n● Open recalls and their progress.\n\nThe same figures shall be available on screen for any date range, filterable by warehouse.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● The daily summary is sent at 07:00 in each warehouse's own time zone.\n● The monthly report is generated automatically on the first day of the month and emailed to the\nconfigured stakeholders.\n● Report figures match the underlying records for the same period.\n● A delivery counts as on time when handed over within 60 minutes of its planned arrival, and as in\nfull when every ordered pack was handed over.\n● Reports can be viewed on screen for any date range and exported.\n● A staff member sees report figures only for their own warehouses.\n\nOut of Scope for Phase 1\n\n● Pricing, invoicing and payments. These remain in Meridian's accounting system.\n● Working out the best order of stops on a route.\n● Purchasing from manufacturers and forecasting demand.\n● Automatic penalties for drivers or customers.\n● Transfers of stock between warehouses.\n● The logger hardware and the supplier's gateway, which already exist.\n</untrusted_document>\n\nWrite the questions."}